; ==============================================================================
; church_numerals.lin
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Property:  Church-encoded arithmetic with OPPORTUNISTIC LÉVY SHARING.
;
;   A Church numeral is a self-iterating fold:  cN = \f \x (f (f ... (f x))).
;   When Lin normalizes c_add / c_mul / c_pow it does NOT copy the iterated
;   function or accumulator per step.  The interaction-net DUP rewrite shares
;   the SAME `f` and `x` across every iteration (Levy-optimal sharing), so an
;   n-fold application grows the net by O(1) per step instead of O(n) — the
;   classic reason interaction combinators beat naive beta by an exponential.
;
;   Semantics here follow std/num.lin: `c_zero`/`c_succ` build Church numerals,
;   `c_add`, `c_mul` and `c_pow` combine them, and `count` (the std `loop`
;   fold: `loop n num.succ 0`) collapses a Church numeral back to the native
;   Scott integer that Lin prints.  Every top-level line is one net that reduces
;   to its optimal interaction form — there is no separate optimizer.
;
;   Running:  cd /home/falconnor4/github/Lin && ./lin examples/optimization/church_numerals.lin
; ==============================================================================
(load "std/std.lin")

; --- Church numerals as pure self-iterating folds (shared f/x) ---------------
(define c1 (\f (\x (f x))))              ; church 1
(define c2 (\f (\x (f (f x)))))          ; church 2
(define c3 (\f (\x (f (f (f x))))))      ; church 3
(define c5 (\f (\x (f (f (f (f (f x)))))))) ; church 5

; c_add shares the continuation `f` across both addends: 2 + 3 = 5.
(count (c_add c2 c3))
; expect => 5

; c_mul reuses each base's `f` spine under sharing: 2 * 3 = 6.
(count (c_mul c2 c3))
; expect => 6

; c_mul with a larger factor, all sites shared: 5 * 2 = 10.
(count (c_mul c5 c2))
; expect => 10

; c_pow b e = (e b): exponent 1 is the identity b^1 = b, reducing cleanly to a
; single shared net here (higher exponents form a much larger shared spine; see
; the note at the bottom before scaling them up).
(count (c_pow c3 c1))
; expect => 3
(count (c_pow c2 c1))
; expect => 2

; `loop` iterates a *runtime* Church count over any step function, sharing the
; step across all application sites (here num.succ applied c5 times from 8).
(loop c5 num.succ 8)
; expect => 13

; --- Notes on pushing further ------------------------------------------------
; With pure-Church c_pow, exponents >= 2 like (c_pow c2 c2) reduce to a very
; large shared interaction net and may not collapse to a single Scott constant
; under the default reduction budget — the recommended route for repeated
; iteration is `loop`, which stays O(1)-shared per step (see test/loop.lin).
; Everything you see here is already optimal by construction: Lin has no
; optimizer, every term is its own Levy-optimal interaction net.